#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/legends.rst:22
msgid "Creating legends"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/legends.rst:24
msgid ""
"The :meth:`pygmt.Figure.legend` method creates legends, whereby auto-legends "
"as well as manually created legends are supported."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/legends.rst:29
msgid ""
"import io\n"
"\n"
"import pygmt\n"
"from pygmt.params import Box, Frame, Position"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/legends.rst:40
msgid "Create an auto-legend"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/legends.rst:42
msgid ""
"An auto-legend can be created for the methods :meth:`pygmt.Figure.plot` and :"
"meth:`pygmt.Figure.plot3d`, :meth:`pygmt.Figure.hlines` and :meth:`pygmt."
"Figure.vlines` as well as :meth:`pygmt.Figure.histogram`. Therefore the "
"``label`` parameter has to be specified to state the desired text for the "
"legend entry (white spaces are supported). Here, we use :meth:`pygmt.Figure."
"plot`, exemplary. By default, the legend is placed in the Upper Right corner "
"and surrounded by a box with a white fill and a 1-point thick, black, solid "
"outline. The order of the legend entries (top to bottom) is determined by "
"the plotting order. Optionally, to adjust the legend, append different "
"modifiers to the string passed to ``label``. For a list of available "
"modifiers see :gmt-docs:`gmt.html#l-full`. To create a :doc:`multiple-column "
"legend </gallery/embellishments/legend>` **+N** is used with the desired "
"number of columns."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/legends.rst:56
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[-5, 5, -5, 5], projection=\"X5c\", frame=True)\n"
"\n"
"# Plot two data points and one line\n"
"fig.plot(x=0, y=0, style=\"c0.2c\", fill=\"orange\", label=\"orange "
"circle\")\n"
"fig.plot(x=1, y=0, style=\"t0.3c\", fill=\"pink\", pen=\"black\", "
"label=\"pink triangle\")\n"
"fig.plot(x=[-3, 3], y=[-2, -2], pen=\"darkred\", label=\"darkred line\")\n"
"\n"
"# Add a legend based on the explanation text given via the \"label\" "
"parameter.\n"
"fig.legend()\n"
"\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/legends.rst:77
msgid "Adjust position and line spacing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/legends.rst:79
msgid ""
"Use the ``position`` parameter to adjust the position of the legend. Note, "
"no box is drawn by default if ``position`` is used"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/legends.rst:84
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[-5, 5, -5, 5], projection=\"X5c\", "
"frame=Frame(axes=\"rltb\"))\n"
"\n"
"fig.plot(x=0, y=0, style=\"c0.25c\", fill=\"orange\", label=\"orange "
"circle\")\n"
"fig.plot(x=1, y=0, style=\"t0.3c\", fill=\"pink\", pen=\"black\", "
"label=\"pink triangle\")\n"
"fig.plot(x=[-3, 3], y=[-2, -2], pen=\"darkred\", label=\"darkred line\")\n"
"\n"
"# Set the reference point to the Top Left corner inside the plot and use "
"offsets of 0.3\n"
"# and 0.2 centimeters in the x- and y-directions, respectively.\n"
"fig.legend(position=Position(\"TL\", offset=(0.3, 0.2)))\n"
"\n"
"fig.shift_origin(xshift=\"w+1c\")\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[-5, 5, -5, 5], projection=\"X5c\", "
"frame=Frame(axes=\"rltb\"))\n"
"\n"
"fig.plot(x=0, y=0, style=\"c0.25c\", fill=\"orange\", label=\"orange "
"circle\")\n"
"fig.plot(x=1, y=0, style=\"t0.3c\", fill=\"pink\", pen=\"black\", "
"label=\"pink triangle\")\n"
"fig.plot(x=[-3, 3], y=[-2, -2], pen=\"darkred\", label=\"darkred line\")\n"
"\n"
"# Use a line spacing factor of 1.5\n"
"fig.legend(position=Position(\"TL\", offset=(0.3, 0.2)), line_spacing=1.5)\n"
"\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/legends.rst:115
msgid "Add a box"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/legends.rst:119
msgid ""
"# Use the ``box`` parameter for adjusting the box around the legend.\n"
"\n"
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.basemap(\n"
"    region=[-5, 5, -5, 5], projection=\"X5c\", frame=Frame(axes=\"rltb\", "
"fill=\"lightgray\")\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"fig.plot(x=0, y=0, style=\"c0.25c\", fill=\"orange\", label=\"orange "
"circle\")\n"
"fig.plot(x=1, y=0, style=\"t0.3c\", fill=\"pink\", pen=\"black\", "
"label=\"pink triangle\")\n"
"fig.plot(x=[-3, 3], y=[-2, -2], pen=\"darkred\", label=\"darkred line\")\n"
"\n"
"fig.legend(position=Position(\"TL\", offset=(0.3, 0.2)), box=True)\n"
"\n"
"fig.shift_origin(xshift=\"w+1c\")\n"
"fig.basemap(\n"
"    region=[-5, 5, -5, 5], projection=\"X5c\", frame=Frame(axes=\"rltb\", "
"fill=\"lightgray\")\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"fig.plot(x=0, y=0, style=\"c0.25c\", fill=\"orange\", label=\"orange "
"circle\")\n"
"fig.plot(x=1, y=0, style=\"t0.3c\", fill=\"pink\", pen=\"black\", "
"label=\"pink triangle\")\n"
"fig.plot(x=[-3, 3], y=[-2, -2], pen=\"darkred\", label=\"darkred line\")\n"
"\n"
"# Add a box with a 2-point thick blue, solid outline and a white fill with "
"a\n"
"# transparency of 30 percent (\"@30\").\n"
"fig.legend(\n"
"    position=Position(\"TL\", offset=(0.3, 0.2)), box=Box(pen=\"2p,blue\", "
"fill=\"white@30\")\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/legends.rst:157
msgid "Create a manual legend"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/legends.rst:159
msgid ""
"For more complicated legends, users need to prepare a legend specification "
"with instructions for the layout of the legend entries. In PyGMT, the legend "
"specification can be either an ASCII file or an :class:`io.StringIO` object. "
"Both are passed to the ``spec`` parameter of :meth:`pygmt.Figure.legend`. "
"Multiple legend codes are available to create complicated legends. In the "
"example below we show an subset; a full overview can be found at :gmt-docs:"
"`legend.html#legend-codes`. It's also supported to include length scales "
"(for geographic projections), faults, and images as well as to add specific "
"lines."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/legends.rst:168
msgid ""
"The following example is orientated on the related GMT example at :gmt-docs:"
"`legend.html#examples`, but modified to use an :class:`io.StringIO` object."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/legends.rst:171
msgid "We start with setting up the :class:`io.StringIO` object."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/legends.rst:175
msgid ""
"spec_io = io.StringIO(\n"
"    \"\"\"\n"
"G -0.1c\n"
"H 24p,Times-Roman My Map Legend\n"
"D 0.2c 1p\n"
"N 2\n"
"V 0 1p\n"
"S 0.1c c 0.20c p300/12 0.25p 0.3c This circle is hachured\n"
"S 0.1c e 0.20c yellow 0.25p 0.3c This ellipse is yellow\n"
"S 0.1c w 0.20c green 0.25p 0.3c This wedge is green\n"
"S 0.1c f 0.25c blue 0.25p 0.3c This is a fault\n"
"S 0.1c - 0.15c - 0.25p,- 0.3c A contour\n"
"S 0.1c v 0.25c magenta 0.5p 0.3c This is a vector\n"
"S 0.1c i 0.20c cyan 0.25p 0.3c This inverse triangle is cyan\n"
"D 0.2c 1p\n"
"V 0 1p\n"
"N 1\n"
"G 0.1c\n"
"M 5 5 600+u+f\n"
"G 0.1c\n"
"I @SOEST_block4.png 3i CT\n"
"G 0.05c\n"
"L 9p,Times-Roman R Smith et al., @%5%J. Geophys. Res., 99@%%, 2000\n"
"G 0.1c\n"
"T Let us just try some simple text that can go on a few lines.\n"
"T There is no easy way to predetermine how many lines may be required\n"
"T so we may have to adjust the height to get the right size box.\n"
"\"\"\"\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/legends.rst:211
msgid ""
"Now, we can add a legend based on this :class:`io.StringIO` object. For "
"multi-columns legends, the width must be specified."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/legends.rst:216
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"# Note, that we are now using a Mercator projection\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[-5, 5, -5, 5], projection=\"M10c\", frame=True)\n"
"# Pass the io.StringIO object to the \"spec\" parameter\n"
"fig.legend(\n"
"    spec=spec_io, position=\"MC\", width=\"9c\", box=Box(pen=\"1p,gray50\", "
"fill=\"gray95\")\n"
")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

